-- COS 101 (Introduction to Computer Science, UNN) practice questions for the CBT feature.
-- 107 questions grounded in the official UNN Computer Science textbook
-- ("Introduction to Computer Science", 2016) and the official UNN COS101
-- course outline. All wording is original.
--
-- Rendering convention (revised): LaTeX is used ONLY where the content is
-- genuinely mathematical (mainly the Number systems topic's binary/octal/
-- hex arithmetic); ordinary prose is left as plain text, not force-wrapped
-- word-by-word. This is simpler and more robust than the earlier "every
-- word in LaTeX" convention, which had caused real rendering bugs
-- (glued-together words, wrong italic font, oversized text) because prose
-- was being routed through the math renderer for no benefit. MathText
-- already natively supports this mixed plain-text/LaTeX style; see
-- lib/core/widgets/math_text.dart.
--
-- cbt_questions has no course_code column, so the COS 101 link lives in
-- subject_name. is_general = false and faculty/department are pinned to
-- 'Faculty of Physical Sciences' / 'Computer Science' so only students in
-- that faculty/department see this paper.

delete from public.cbt_questions where subject_id = 'cos-101-intro-computing';

insert into public.cbt_questions
  (subject_id, subject_name, institution, faculty, department, level,
   question, options, correct_index, explanation, topic, is_general)
values
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A computer system is best defined as a machine that operates under the control of instructions stored in its own memory and can:', '["only display pre-recorded video content without ever accepting new data or instructions", "capture/accept data, process it according to rules, produce results, and store them for future use", "only store files permanently without ever processing or transforming the data it holds", "only connect separate computers together over a network without doing any computation itself"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A computer (or computer system) is an electronic machine operating under the control of instructions stored in its own memory, which can capture/accept data, manipulate the data according to specified rules, produce results, and store the results for future use.', 'Concepts of hardware and software', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In computing, the physical, touchable parts of a computer system (the keyboard, monitor, CPU chip, disk drives, etc.) are collectively called:', '["software", "firmware only", "hardware", "data"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hardware refers to the physical components of a computer system — the parts you can physically touch, such as the keyboard, monitor, processor chip and storage devices.', 'Concepts of hardware and software', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Software can be broadly defined as:', '["a set of computer programs (and possibly associated data) held in storage that instructs the computer to perform a specific task", "any electronic device that is physically plugged into the computer''s ports to expand its capability", "the metal casing and internal frame that physically protects the computer''s electronic components", "only the operating system, since every other program is really considered part of the hardware"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Computer software is a set of computer programs, and possibly associated data, held in computer storage, such that the processor may execute the programs (and use the data) to perform some specific purpose.', 'Concepts of hardware and software', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A computer program is best described as:', '["a hardware component that stores data permanently even when the computer''s power is switched off", "a sequence of instructions to be followed by the CPU to perform a specific task", "a physical input device used to enter commands directly into the computer''s memory", "the physical wiring and circuitry inside the CPU that carries its electrical signals"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A computer program is a sequence of instructions written to be followed by the CPU in order to perform a specific task. The person who writes the program is called a programmer.', 'Concepts of hardware and software', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The form in which a program is written by a programmer, before it is transformed into the CPU''s native machine language, is called:', '["object code", "source code", "firmware", "binary code"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A program in the form in which it is written by the programmer is called source code. For the CPU to follow (execute) the program, the source code must first be transformed into the native machine language of that CPU.', 'Concepts of hardware and software', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which relationship correctly describes how software relates to a computer program and its data?', '["software = program(s) + data always combined, meaning a program can never exist or run without its own dedicated data", "software = program(s) [+ data], meaning software consists of one or more programs, optionally together with associated data", "software is unrelated to programs entirely; software refers only to the physical media the programs happen to be stored on", "software is only the data files a computer holds, never the instructions that tell it what to do with them"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Software can be expressed as software = program(s) [+ data] — software is made up of one or more programs, and may optionally include the data those programs work with.', 'Concepts of hardware and software', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which of the following is an example of application software rather than systems software?', '["the operating system that manages the computer''s hardware resources", "a compiler that translates source code into machine language", "a word processor used to write a letter", "a device driver that lets the operating system control a printer"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Systems software (like the operating system, compilers, and device drivers) exists to run and manage the computer itself. A word processor is application software — it exists to help the user accomplish a specific real-world task, such as writing a document.', 'Concepts of hardware and software', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A program is called a systems program if it performs general duties needed for the execution of other programs. Which of these is a systems program?', '["a payroll application used by a company''s accounts department to calculate staff salaries", "an examination marking program used by a school to grade multiple-choice test scripts", "a program that allocates memory space and CPU time to other running programs", "a spreadsheet application used to prepare and track a household or business budget"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A systems program performs general duties in support of other programs — for example, allocating memory space and CPU time, or providing printing services — rather than solving a specific end-user task directly.', 'Concepts of hardware and software', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which of the following best describes the relationship between hardware and software in a working computer system?', '["Software is completely independent of hardware and can run without any physical machine", "Hardware alone, without any software, is what most users interact with directly", "Software provides the instructions that the hardware carries out; the two work together to accomplish tasks", "Hardware is a subtype of software, since every physical component is really a program running in the background"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hardware is the physical machine, but on its own it can only follow instructions — those instructions are the software. The two work together: software directs what the hardware should do.', 'Concepts of hardware and software', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The unit of a digital computer that performs arithmetic operations (like addition and subtraction) and logical operations (like comparisons) is the:', '["Control Unit (CU), which only directs and sequences other components", "Arithmetic and Logic Unit (ALU)", "Input unit, which only accepts data from outside the computer", "Output unit, which only presents already-processed results"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Arithmetic and Logic Unit (ALU) is the subsystem responsible for carrying out arithmetic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division) and logical operations (such as AND, OR, and comparisons like greater-than/less-than).', 'Basic subsystems of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which subsystem directs and coordinates the operations of the entire computer, fetching instructions from memory and telling other units what to do?', '["Control Unit (CU)", "Secondary storage", "Arithmetic and Logic Unit (ALU)", "Output unit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Control Unit (CU) directs the operation of the computer: it fetches instructions from memory, decodes them, and coordinates/directs the ALU, memory, and input/output units to carry each instruction out.', 'Basic subsystems of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The Control Unit (CU) and the Arithmetic and Logic Unit (ALU), taken together, form the:', '["input unit, which only captures data entered by the user", "Central Processing Unit (CPU)", "output unit, which only displays or prints results", "secondary storage unit, which only retains data long-term"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Central Processing Unit (CPU) is made up of the Control Unit, which directs operations, and the Arithmetic and Logic Unit, which performs computations — together they form the ''brain'' of the computer.', 'Basic subsystems of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Primary memory (main memory) in a digital computer is used to:', '["permanently archive data for years even with the power switched off completely", "temporarily hold data and instructions that the CPU is currently working with", "convert analogue electrical signals into digital signals for the processor only", "physically print processed output onto paper for the user to keep"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Primary (main) memory temporarily holds the data and instructions the CPU needs while actively processing a task. Unlike secondary storage, most primary memory (RAM) loses its contents when power is removed.', 'Basic subsystems of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which of these best matches the four basic functional subsystems commonly identified in a digital computer''s architecture?', '["input, processing (CPU/memory), output, and storage", "keyboard, mouse, monitor, and printer only", "hardware, software, firmware, and middleware", "binary, octal, decimal, and hexadecimal"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A digital computer''s basic subsystems are commonly grouped as: an input subsystem (captures data), a processing subsystem (CPU and memory, which computes results), an output subsystem (presents results), and a storage subsystem (retains data/programs).', 'Basic subsystems of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In the basic data-flow model of a computer, the correct general order of operation is:', '["output is generated first, then the data is processed, and only afterward is it input", "input data enters, the CPU processes it using memory, and results are sent to output (with storage available throughout)", "data is placed into storage before any input is ever collected from the user", "the ALU produces finished output directly, with no control unit involvement at all"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Data first enters the system through an input device, the CPU (control unit and ALU, aided by memory) processes it, and the results are then presented through an output device; storage devices can save data/programs before, during, or after this cycle.', 'Basic subsystems of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A bus, in computer architecture, refers to:', '["a type of printer that transfers ink from a cartridge onto paper", "a set of parallel wires/pathways that carry data, addresses, or control signals between components of the computer", "the plastic casing that forms the outer shell of the system unit", "a systems program that manages files stored on the hard disk"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A bus is a common pathway (a set of conductors) along which data, memory addresses, or control signals travel between the CPU, memory, and other components inside a computer.', 'Basic subsystems of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which best describes the purpose of the system unit''s motherboard?', '["It is a purely decorative cover fitted over the outside of the computer case", "It is the main circuit board that houses/connects the CPU, memory, and other core components via buses and slots", "It is a type of input device used to type commands directly into the computer", "It is a category of application software used to manage office documents"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The motherboard is the main printed circuit board of a computer; the CPU, memory chips, and expansion cards plug into it, and its buses/circuitry allow these components to communicate with each other.', 'Basic subsystems of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which of the following is NOT one of the widely recognised general characteristics of a digital computer?', '["Speed — it performs operations in fractions of a second", "Accuracy — it produces error-free results provided correct data and instructions are given", "Diligence — it can perform repetitive tasks without getting tired or losing consistency", "Independent judgement — it can decide on its own what task to perform without any instructions"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A computer has no independent judgement or initiative: it can only do exactly what it is instructed to do through a program, following the well-known principle ''garbage in, garbage out''. Its recognised characteristics are speed, accuracy, diligence, storage capacity, and versatility — not the ability to decide tasks on its own.', 'Characteristics of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The characteristic of a computer that describes its ability to perform millions of instructions every second is called:', '["versatility", "speed", "diligence", "storage"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Speed refers to a computer''s ability to process enormous numbers of instructions within a very short time, typically measured in operations per second (e.g. millions or billions of instructions per second).', 'Characteristics of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A computer''s ability to perform a wide variety of different tasks — from word processing to calculations to playing media — is known as its:', '["versatility", "rigidity", "accuracy", "capacity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Versatility describes a computer''s capability to carry out many different kinds of tasks, simply by running different programs, rather than being limited to a single fixed function.', 'Characteristics of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Unlike a human, a computer can repeat the same task thousands of times without a drop in accuracy or the need to rest. This characteristic is called:', '["diligence", "creativity", "emotion", "intuition"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Diligence describes a computer''s ability to perform the same repetitive task over and over, for as long as required, without becoming tired, bored, or less accurate — something humans cannot sustain indefinitely.', 'Characteristics of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The saying ''Garbage In, Garbage Out'' (GIGO) in computing means that:', '["computers can automatically detect and correct wrong data before processing it further", "the quality/correctness of a computer''s output depends entirely on the quality/correctness of the input and instructions it was given", "old, slower computers always produce worse results than newer ones, regardless of the input", "computers physically discard bad data as waste output rather than processing it at all"]'::jsonb, 1, 'GIGO reflects the fact that a computer has no judgement of its own: if it is given incorrect or poor-quality data or instructions, it will faithfully process them and produce incorrect or poor-quality results.', 'Characteristics of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which characteristic of computers refers to their ability to hold enormous amounts of data and instructions for later retrieval?', '["storage capacity", "speed", "diligence", "versatility"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Storage capacity refers to a computer''s ability to store vast quantities of data and instructions, which can later be retrieved as needed, in devices such as hard disks and other secondary storage media.', 'Characteristics of a digital computer', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which of these is primarily an input device rather than an output device?', '["monitor", "plotter", "scanner", "speaker"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A scanner captures an image of a physical document and converts it into digital data that the computer can process — that makes it an input device. Monitors, plotters, and speakers all present processed results to the user, making them output devices.', 'Devices/media', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A device that allows a user to point, click, and select items on the screen by moving it across a flat surface is called a:', '["mouse", "plotter", "modem", "hard disk"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A mouse is a pointing input device; moving it across a surface moves an on-screen pointer, and its buttons let the user select or activate items.', 'Devices/media', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which input device is specifically designed to convert sound (such as a user''s voice) into an electrical/digital signal the computer can process?', '["microphone", "joystick", "light pen", "digital camera"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A microphone is an input device that captures sound waves (such as speech) and converts them into an electrical signal, which the computer''s sound hardware then digitises for processing or storage.', 'Devices/media', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A CRT (Cathode Ray Tube) monitor and an LCD (Liquid Crystal Display) monitor are both examples of:', '["input devices used to capture data from the user", "output devices used to visually display processed information", "primary storage devices used to hold data temporarily", "types of printers that produce output on paper"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both CRT and LCD monitors are visual display units (VDUs) — output devices that present the computer''s processed results to the user as images on a screen, though they use different underlying display technologies.', 'Devices/media', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A device that produces high-quality line drawings, engineering diagrams, or large-format graphics by moving a pen (or similar marking tool) across paper is called a:', '["plotter", "scanner", "keyboard", "joystick"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A plotter is an output device that draws images by moving a pen, or an equivalent marking mechanism, across the surface of paper — commonly used for precise line-art such as architectural or engineering drawings.', 'Devices/media', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'RAM (Random Access Memory) is best described as:', '["permanent, non-volatile storage that keeps data even when the power is off", "volatile primary memory whose contents are lost when power is removed", "an output device for printing documents", "a type of secondary storage medium like a hard disk"]'::jsonb, 1, 'RAM is volatile primary memory: the CPU can read from and write to it very quickly while the computer is on, but its contents are lost as soon as power is removed — unlike secondary storage such as a hard disk.', 'Devices/media', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'ROM (Read-Only Memory) differs from RAM mainly because ROM:', '["can be freely rewritten by any running application at any time, just like RAM", "semi-permanently holds data such as startup instructions, cannot be changed by ordinary programs, and keeps its contents without power", "is always physically larger in storage capacity than the computer''s RAM", "is a type of output device used to present processed results to the user"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ROM holds data — often firmware or startup instructions — that is written once (or rarely) and is not meant to be altered by normal running programs; crucially, unlike RAM, it is non-volatile, so it retains its contents even when the power is off.', 'Devices/media', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which of these is a secondary (auxiliary) storage device rather than a primary memory type?', '["RAM", "cache memory", "hard disk", "register"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A hard disk is a secondary storage device: it stores data and programs permanently (until deliberately deleted) in large capacities, and is used to hold data even when the computer is switched off — unlike RAM, cache, and registers, which are all primary/internal memory.', 'Devices/media', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'One important reason to properly ''safely remove'' or ''eject'' a USB flash drive before physically unplugging it is to:', '["increase the drive''s total available storage capacity", "ensure that all pending write operations have finished, preventing data corruption or loss", "recharge the flash drive''s internal battery before storing it", "convert the files stored on it to a different file format"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The operating system may still be writing data to a USB flash drive even after a copy appears to finish. Safely ejecting it ensures all pending writes are completed and the connection is closed cleanly, preventing corrupted or lost data.', 'Devices/media', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which practice best helps in caring for and prolonging the life of a hard disk?', '["Frequently exposing the drive to strong magnetic fields near its casing", "Shutting the computer down abruptly by cutting power while it is still writing data", "Shutting the system down properly, avoiding physical shocks/vibration, and keeping it away from dust and extreme heat", "Storing the computer in a very humid, dusty environment for long periods"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hard disks contain sensitive mechanical and magnetic components; proper shutdown procedures, avoiding physical shocks or vibration while running, and keeping the drive away from dust, excess heat, and magnetic fields all help protect stored data and extend the disk''s working life.', 'Devices/media', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A Personal Computer (PC) is best classified, in terms of computer size/power categories, as a:', '["supercomputer", "mainframe computer", "microcomputer", "minicomputer only used by large organisations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A microcomputer is a small, relatively low-cost computer built around a microprocessor, designed for use by one person at a time — the Personal Computer (PC) is the most common example of this category.', 'Types/classes of computer emphasising the PC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The fastest and most powerful class of computers, typically used for extremely complex tasks such as weather forecasting and nuclear simulations, is called:', '["supercomputers", "microcomputers", "embedded computers", "personal digital assistants"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Supercomputers are the most powerful class of computers, capable of extremely high-speed processing, and are used for computation-intensive tasks like weather forecasting, scientific simulations, and cryptography — well beyond the needs of an individual user.', 'Types/classes of computer emphasising the PC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A large, powerful computer used by big organisations (such as banks) to process huge volumes of transactions and serve many users/terminals simultaneously is called a:', '["mainframe computer", "laptop", "microcomputer", "tablet"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A mainframe computer is a large-scale, high-capacity computer used by organisations to process large volumes of data and serve numerous simultaneous users/terminals, such as in banking or airline reservation systems.', 'Types/classes of computer emphasising the PC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A desktop PC, a laptop, and a tablet computer are all examples of which class of computer?', '["microcomputers", "mainframes", "minicomputers used only in industrial control rooms", "supercomputers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Desktops, laptops, and tablets are all microcomputers — general-purpose computers built around a single microprocessor, intended for use by an individual user, differing mainly in their form factor and portability.', 'Types/classes of computer emphasising the PC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which of these is the best distinguishing feature of a laptop computer compared to a desktop PC?', '["A laptop integrates the system unit, screen, keyboard, and battery into one portable unit, while a desktop''s components are typically separate and mains-powered", "A laptop cannot run any application software that a desktop PC is able to run", "A laptop has no processor and simply displays output from a separate desktop machine", "A desktop is always more portable and lighter to carry around than a laptop"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A laptop is a portable microcomputer that combines the processor, memory, screen, keyboard, and a rechargeable battery in a single foldable unit, whereas a desktop PC typically has separate components (system unit, monitor, keyboard) and relies on mains power.', 'Types/classes of computer emphasising the PC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'According to the officially credited timeline, the first true personal computer to closely resemble what we now think of as a modern portable/personal computer was pioneered by projects such as the Xerox Alto, while the first commercially significant IBM-compatible PC was released by IBM in:', '["1971", "1981", "1995", "2001"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The IBM Personal Computer (IBM PC), released in 1981, is widely credited as the first PC-compatible machine, and it established the hardware/software standard that later ''PC clone'' manufacturers (like Compaq in 1983) would follow.', 'History of computers and their generations', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The very first PC clone (a non-IBM machine that was compatible with the IBM PC standard) was released by Compaq in:', '["1976", "1981", "1983", "1990"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Compaq released the first true IBM PC-compatible clone in March 1983, proving that other manufacturers could legally build machines compatible with IBM''s PC standard — a landmark moment that opened up the personal computer market to competition.', 'History of computers and their generations', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The Apple I, credited as one of the first personal computers created by Steve Wozniak (with Steve Jobs), was introduced in:', '["1969", "1976", "1985", "1993"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Apple I, designed largely by Steve Wozniak, was introduced by Apple Computer in 1976, marking Apple''s entry into the personal computer market.', 'History of computers and their generations', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The abacus, one of the earliest known devices used to aid counting and calculation, originates from ancient civilisations and predates electronic computers by:', '["a few years", "thousands of years", "a few decades", "It does not predate electronic computers"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The abacus is one of the oldest known calculating tools, used by ancient civilisations (including the Chinese) thousands of years before the invention of any electronic computer, making it a foundational milestone in the pre-history of computing.', 'History of computers and their generations', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'John Napier, a Scottish mathematician, made an important early contribution to computing history by inventing:', '["the microprocessor used in modern electronic computers", "Napier''s Bones, a manual calculating tool that simplified multiplication and division", "the modern electronic transistor used in second-generation computers", "the first high-level programming language used to write software"]'::jsonb, 1, 'John Napier invented ''Napier''s Bones'', a set of numbered rods that could be used to perform multiplication and division mechanically — an important early aid to calculation that came well before electronic computers.', 'History of computers and their generations', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Alan Turing''s theoretical ''Turing machine'', described in 1936, is historically significant in computing mainly because it:', '["was a physical PC sold commercially to members of the public", "provided a formal mathematical model of computation that underlies the theory of what any computer can compute", "was the first hard disk drive installed inside a working computer", "was a type of printer used for high-speed line drawings"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Alan Turing''s 1936 concept of the Turing machine is a theoretical/mathematical model describing computation in the abstract; it laid the foundational theory for what is computable, deeply influencing the later design and theory of real computers.', 'History of computers and their generations', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The transition from vacuum tubes to transistors marks the boundary between which two generations of computers?', '["third generation to fourth generation", "first generation to second generation", "fourth generation to fifth generation", "There was never such a transition"]'::jsonb, 1, 'First-generation computers used vacuum tubes as their switching components; second-generation computers replaced these with transistors, which were smaller, more reliable, generated less heat, and consumed less power.', 'History of computers and their generations', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Early machines such as EDVAC and the Manchester Baby, which used magnetic core memory and stored-program concepts, belong to which generation of computers?', '["first generation (vacuum-tube era)", "fourth generation (microprocessor era)", "fifth generation (AI era)", "None — they were never built"]'::jsonb, 0, 'EDVAC and the Manchester Baby were pioneering first-generation, stored-program computers built in the vacuum-tube era, and their design (including early magnetic core memory concepts) heavily influenced the architecture of computers that followed.', 'History of computers and their generations', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In problem solving with computers, an algorithm is best defined as:', '["a finite, well-defined, ordered sequence of steps that solves a specific problem", "a type of computer hardware installed inside the system unit", "a random guess at a solution, made without any planning at all", "the final printed output produced once a program has finished running"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An algorithm is a finite, unambiguous, ordered sequence of steps that, when followed, solves a specific problem or accomplishes a specific task — it exists independently of any particular programming language.', 'Problem-solving: algorithm, flowchart, pseudocode', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Pseudocode is best described as:', '["an informal, English-like way of expressing an algorithm''s logic without following the strict syntax rules of any real programming language", "a diagram made only of geometric shapes and directional arrows, with no written words at all", "the low-level machine code produced automatically after a program has been compiled", "a physical hardware component located inside the CPU chip itself"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pseudocode expresses the logical steps of an algorithm in structured, plain, English-like statements, letting a programmer plan a solution''s logic without worrying about the precise syntax rules of a specific programming language.', 'Problem-solving: algorithm, flowchart, pseudocode', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A flowchart represents an algorithm using:', '["standardised geometric symbols (such as ovals, rectangles, diamonds) connected by arrows to show the sequence and flow of steps", "only spoken word descriptions read aloud, with no diagrams of any kind", "binary machine code that the processor executes directly without translation", "a spreadsheet of numbers only, with no symbols or arrows involved"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A flowchart is a diagrammatic representation of an algorithm, using standard symbols — for example, ovals for start/end (terminators), rectangles for processing steps, and diamonds for decisions — linked by arrows that show the order in which steps are carried out.', 'Problem-solving: algorithm, flowchart, pseudocode', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In standard flowchart notation, which symbol shape is used to represent a decision (a point where the flow branches based on a condition, e.g. yes/no)?', '["an oval", "a rectangle", "a diamond (rhombus)", "a parallelogram"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A diamond (rhombus) shape represents a decision point in a flowchart — the flow branches into two or more paths depending on whether a stated condition is true or false.', 'Problem-solving: algorithm, flowchart, pseudocode', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In standard flowchart notation, an oval (rounded) shape is conventionally used to represent:', '["a processing or calculation step performed part-way through the algorithm", "the start or end (terminator) of the flowchart", "an input or output operation such as reading or displaying a value", "a loop counter used to track how many times a loop has repeated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The oval (or rounded rectangle) is the standard terminator symbol in flowcharting, used to mark the single starting point (''Start'') and the ending point(s) (''Stop''/''End'') of an algorithm.', 'Problem-solving: algorithm, flowchart, pseudocode', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', '''Stepwise refinement'', as a problem-solving technique in programming, means:', '["writing the entire complex program in one single step with no planning beforehand", "breaking a complex problem down gradually into smaller and smaller, more manageable sub-steps until each is simple enough to implement directly", "removing all the comments from a finished, already-working program", "converting a finished program from one programming language into another"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Stepwise refinement (also called top-down design) is the technique of starting with a broad statement of a problem and progressively breaking it into smaller, more detailed sub-steps, refining each one further until every step is simple and precise enough to be coded directly.', 'Problem-solving: algorithm, flowchart, pseudocode', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which of the following correctly orders the typical stages of solving a problem with a computer, from first to last?', '["Coding first, then problem definition, then algorithm design, then testing", "Problem definition/analysis, algorithm/flowchart design, coding (writing the program), then testing and debugging", "Testing first, then coding the program, then finally defining the problem", "There is no meaningful order at all; every stage happens simultaneously"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The standard problem-solving process is: first understand and define the problem, then design a solution as an algorithm or flowchart, then translate that design into actual program code, and finally test and debug the program to confirm it works correctly.', 'Problem-solving: algorithm, flowchart, pseudocode', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A rectangle shape in standard flowchart notation is used to represent:', '["a decision point where the flow branches based on a condition", "a general processing step, such as a calculation or an assignment", "the start of the flowchart only, never anything else", "an external data source located outside the algorithm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A rectangle is the standard flowchart symbol for a processing step — an operation such as a calculation, or assigning a value to a variable — as opposed to input/output or a decision.', 'Problem-solving: algorithm, flowchart, pseudocode', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In the BASIC programming language, which statement is used to display output to the screen, such as printing the value of a variable?', '["INPUT", "PRINT", "DIM", "GOTO"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The PRINT statement in BASIC is used to display text or the value of a variable/expression on the screen as output.', 'Programming: introduction to Java/BASIC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In BASIC, the statement used to accept a value typed by the user at the keyboard and store it in a variable is:', '["INPUT", "PRINT", "END", "REM"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The INPUT statement in BASIC pauses the program, waits for the user to type a value at the keyboard, and stores whatever is typed into the named variable.', 'Programming: introduction to Java/BASIC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In BASIC''s standard operator precedence, when an expression contains both exponentiation (^) and multiplication (*), which operation is performed first?', '["Multiplication is always performed before exponentiation in every expression", "Exponentiation (^) is performed before multiplication, following BASIC''s standard order of precedence", "They are always performed strictly left to right, regardless of which operator appears", "BASIC does not support exponentiation as an operator at all"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In BASIC''s standard precedence rules, exponentiation (^) has a higher priority than multiplication and division, which in turn have higher priority than addition and subtraction — so exponentiation is evaluated first unless parentheses dictate otherwise.', 'Programming: introduction to Java/BASIC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The BASIC function RANDOMIZE is typically used to:', '["sort an array of numbers into ascending or descending order", "seed/reinitialise the random-number generator so a program''s sequence of ''random'' numbers varies between runs", "permanently delete a variable from the running program", "send a document to the printer for a paper copy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'RANDOMIZE is used in BASIC to seed the random-number generator (often using the system clock), so that each time the program runs, its sequence of pseudo-random numbers (generated by functions like RND) differs rather than repeating identically.', 'Programming: introduction to Java/BASIC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In BASIC, an IF...THEN...ELSE statement is used to:', '["declare the size of an array before values are stored in it", "make the program choose between two alternative sets of actions, depending on whether a stated condition is true or false", "always execute both branches together, regardless of the condition''s value", "input data from a file only, never from the keyboard"]'::jsonb, 1, 'IF...THEN...ELSE is BASIC''s conditional (selection) statement: if the condition is true, the THEN branch runs; if it is false, the ELSE branch runs instead — allowing the program to make a decision.', 'Programming: introduction to Java/BASIC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In the Java programming language, which of these is a valid, legal identifier (variable name)?', '["2ndValue", "total-sum", "totalSum", "class"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Java identifiers must start with a letter, underscore, or dollar sign (never a digit), cannot contain characters like a hyphen, and cannot be one of Java''s reserved keywords (like ''class''). ''totalSum'' satisfies all these rules.', 'Programming: introduction to Java/BASIC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In Java, which primitive data type would be most appropriate for storing a single true/false value?', '["int", "boolean", "char", "float"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Java''s boolean type holds exactly one of two literal values, true or false, making it the correct choice for representing a logical true/false condition.', 'Programming: introduction to Java/BASIC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In Java''s object-oriented model, a class is best described as:', '["a blueprint/template that defines the data members (fields) and methods that objects created from it will have", "a single numeric value stored temporarily while a program is running", "a type of input device used to enter commands into a program", "a loop construct used to repeat a block of statements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A class in Java is a template or blueprint: it defines what data members (fields/attributes) and methods (behaviours) objects of that class will possess. Individual objects are then created (''instantiated'') from the class.', 'Programming: introduction to Java/BASIC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In Java''s inheritance model, if class Dog extends class Animal, then Dog is called the ___ and Animal is called the ___.', '["interface; package", "subclass; superclass", "superclass; subclass", "method; object"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In Java inheritance, the class that inherits (Dog, using ''extends'') is called the subclass, while the class it inherits from (Animal) is called the superclass. The subclass automatically gains the superclass''s accessible fields and methods.', 'Programming: introduction to Java/BASIC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which of the following is a valid Java boolean literal?', '["\"true\"", "True", "true", "1"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In Java, the boolean literals are exactly the lowercase keywords true and false. "true" (in quotes) is a String, True is not a recognised keyword (Java is case-sensitive), and 1 is an int, not a boolean.', 'Programming: introduction to Java/BASIC', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Starting up a computer that is completely powered off, running its full Power-On Self-Test (POST) and loading the operating system from scratch, is known as a:', '["warm boot", "cold boot", "soft shutdown", "hibernation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A cold boot happens when a computer is started from a completely powered-off state: the hardware runs its full Power-On Self-Test (POST) before the operating system is loaded, unlike a warm boot which restarts an already-powered computer.', 'Booting and shutdown', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Restarting a computer that is already switched on (for example, using a ''Restart'' menu option) without turning the power completely off first is known as a:', '["cold boot from a fully powered-off state", "warm boot (or ''warm reboot'')", "factory reset that erases all user data", "disk defragmentation of the hard drive"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A warm boot restarts a computer that is already powered on, typically skipping some of the hardware checks a full cold boot performs, and is commonly triggered through the operating system''s own restart command.', 'Booting and shutdown', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'During booting, the small program stored in ROM/firmware that performs initial hardware checks and then locates and loads the operating system is generally known as the:', '["word processor used to type and format documents", "BIOS/bootloader (boot firmware)", "web browser used to access pages on the internet", "spreadsheet macro used to automate repeated calculations"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The BIOS (or, on modern systems, UEFI firmware) performs the Power-On Self-Test and then hands control to a bootloader, which locates and loads the operating system into memory — this sequence is what allows the computer to become usable after being switched on.', 'Booting and shutdown', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Why is it recommended to always shut a computer down properly through the operating system''s shutdown command, rather than simply cutting off the power?', '["It makes no real difference either way, since both methods stop the computer identically", "A proper shutdown lets the operating system close open files and save unsaved data safely, reducing the risk of data loss or file/disk corruption", "Cutting the power abruptly always makes the computer start up noticeably faster next time", "A proper shutdown physically empties and erases the entire contents of the hard disk"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A proper operating-system shutdown closes running programs, flushes any data still waiting to be written to disk, and safely powers down hardware components — abruptly cutting power instead risks losing unsaved work or corrupting files/the file system.', 'Booting and shutdown', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'An operating system designed to allow only one user to work on the computer, running one task/program actively at a time, is classified as:', '["single-user, single-task", "multi-user, multi-task", "real-time, multi-user", "network operating system only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A single-user, single-task operating system allows only one user on the machine at a time and lets that user run only one program at a time — an early, simple category of operating system design.', 'Types of operating system', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'An operating system that allows one user to run several different programs seemingly at the same time (e.g. browsing the web while music plays in the background) is classified as:', '["single-user, multi-tasking", "single-user, single-task only", "batch processing only", "real-time only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A single-user, multi-tasking operating system (like most modern desktop operating systems) is used by one person at a time, but allows that person to run multiple programs concurrently, switching between them as needed.', 'Types of operating system', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'An operating system that allows many different users, at different terminals, to access the same computer''s resources at the same time is classified as a:', '["multi-user operating system", "single-task operating system", "embedded operating system only", "None of the above — this is impossible"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A multi-user operating system manages resources so that two or more users can access the computer''s processing power and resources simultaneously, typically from separate terminals or connections — common on servers and mainframes.', 'Types of operating system', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A real-time operating system is specifically characterised by its ability to:', '["process and respond to inputs within a strict, guaranteed, very short time limit, which is critical for tasks like industrial control systems", "only run a single program once every week, regardless of demand", "ignore all timing requirements entirely and respond whenever convenient", "run exclusively on supercomputers, never on smaller dedicated devices"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A real-time operating system guarantees that it processes input and produces a response within a strict, predictable, and very short time constraint — essential in applications like industrial control systems, medical equipment, or robotics, where delayed responses could be dangerous.', 'Types of operating system', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A Command-Line Interface (CLI) differs from a Graphical User Interface (GUI) mainly in that a CLI:', '["requires the user to type text commands, while a GUI lets the user interact using windows, icons, menus, and a pointing device", "can only ever be used together with a mouse, and never with a keyboard", "is always faster and easier to learn for a first-time computer user than a GUI is", "has no relationship to the operating system whatsoever, unlike a GUI"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A Command-Line Interface requires the user to type precise textual commands to interact with the operating system, whereas a Graphical User Interface lets the user interact visually through windows, icons, menus, and pointers (like a mouse) — generally considered more intuitive for beginners.', 'User interface', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The user interface element that represents a program, file, or function using a small picture, which the user can click or double-click to activate, is called a(n):', '["icon", "kernel", "compiler", "protocol"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An icon is a small graphical symbol used in a graphical user interface to represent a program, file, folder, or function, which the user can select or activate (often by clicking or double-clicking) rather than typing a command.', 'User interface', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A drop-down list of commands or options that appears when a user clicks on a heading (such as ''File'' or ''Edit'') in a GUI application''s top bar is called a:', '["taskbar", "menu", "cursor", "scrollbar"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A menu is a GUI element that presents a list of selectable commands or options to the user, typically appearing when the user clicks a heading like ''File'' or ''Edit'' in an application''s menu bar.', 'User interface', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Malicious software that attaches itself to legitimate files/programs and can replicate itself by infecting other files when the host program runs is generally known as a computer:', '["virus", "spreadsheet", "compiler", "peripheral"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A computer virus is a type of malware that attaches itself to a legitimate host file or program and spreads by infecting other files whenever the infected program is executed, potentially damaging data or disrupting the system.', 'Managing information, computer malware', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Unlike a virus, a computer worm is distinguished mainly by its ability to:', '["spread and replicate across networks and computers on its own, without needing to attach itself to a host file or needing a user to run an infected program", "only affect printed documents that have already left the printer", "improve a computer''s processing speed by optimising its memory usage", "physically damage the keys and casing of the computer''s keyboard"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A worm is a self-replicating malware program that can spread across networks and systems entirely on its own, without needing to attach to a host file or requiring a user to execute an infected program — unlike a classic virus.', 'Managing information, computer malware', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Malware that disguises itself as a legitimate or useful program, but secretly carries out harmful actions once run, is known as a:', '["Trojan horse", "firewall", "antivirus program", "backup utility"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A Trojan horse is malware that disguises itself as legitimate or desirable software; once a user is tricked into running it, it carries out hidden, harmful actions — unlike a virus or worm, it typically does not self-replicate.', 'Managing information, computer malware', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Malware that secretly monitors a user''s activity (such as keystrokes or browsing habits) and sends that information to a third party without consent is called:', '["spyware", "a database management system", "a plotter driver", "an operating system kernel"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Spyware is malicious software that covertly gathers information about a user''s activity, such as keystrokes, browsing history, or personal data, and transmits it to a third party without the user''s knowledge or consent.', 'Managing information, computer malware', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which of the following is a recommended basic practice for managing information and protecting a computer against malware?', '["Regularly opening email attachments from unknown senders without checking them", "Keeping antivirus software installed and updated, backing up important data regularly, and avoiding untrusted downloads/links", "Disabling all security software so that the computer runs somewhat faster", "Sharing passwords freely with anyone who happens to ask for them"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Good information-management/security practice includes keeping antivirus software updated so it can detect newer threats, regularly backing up important data in case of infection or failure, and avoiding suspicious downloads, links, or attachments that commonly deliver malware.', 'Managing information, computer malware', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A computer network in which all devices connect to and communicate along one single shared central cable is described as having which topology?', '["star topology", "bus topology", "ring topology", "mesh topology"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In a bus topology, every device is connected to one single shared central cable (the ''bus''), and data sent by one device travels along this shared cable to reach the others.', 'Computer network', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In a star network topology, all the devices on the network are connected:', '["directly to a single central device (such as a switch or hub), rather than directly to each other", "in a single closed loop, with data passing around it and no central device at all", "to every other device individually, with cabling but no central connecting point", "using only wireless satellite links, with no cabling anywhere in the network"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In a star topology, every device connects individually to a central device (like a hub or switch); all communication between devices passes through this central point rather than device-to-device directly.', 'Computer network', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In a ring network topology, each device is connected to exactly two neighbouring devices, forming:', '["a closed loop (circle) through which data typically travels in one direction around the ring", "a tree-shaped hierarchy with a single root device at the very top", "a completely random arrangement with no predictable connection pattern", "a single point-to-point link connecting only two devices in total"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A ring topology connects each device to exactly two other devices, forming a closed loop; data is typically passed from device to device around the ring until it reaches its destination.', 'Computer network', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A Local Area Network (LAN) is best described as a network that:', '["spans an entire continent, connecting cities across several countries", "connects computers and devices within a small, limited geographical area, such as a single building or campus", "only ever connects exactly two computers, however far apart they are", "has no practical size limit at all, regardless of distance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A LAN connects computers and devices within a relatively small, confined geographic area, such as a single office, building, or campus — as opposed to a Wide Area Network (WAN), which spans much larger distances, even entire countries or continents.', 'Computer network', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Real-time audio-and-video communication between two or more people at different locations, using computers/devices over a network, is generally called:', '["teleconferencing (videoconferencing)", "disk formatting of a storage device", "flowcharting an algorithm''s logic", "number-base conversion between systems"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Teleconferencing (specifically videoconferencing when it includes live video) allows people at different physical locations to communicate in real time over a network using audio, and often video, rather than meeting in person.', 'Computer network', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A URL (Uniform Resource Locator), such as https://www.example.com/page.html, is best described as:', '["a type of computer virus that spreads through email attachments", "the standardised address used to locate a specific resource (such as a webpage) on the internet", "a reserved keyword belonging to a particular programming language", "a type of malware that secretly monitors a user''s activity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A URL is a standardised text address that specifies exactly where a particular resource (such as a webpage, image, or file) can be found on the internet, typically including the protocol, domain name, and path.', 'Internet and web usage, email, instant messaging', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'HTTP, the protocol used to transfer webpages between a web server and a web browser, stands for:', '["HyperText Transfer Protocol", "High-Traffic Transmission Program", "Home Terminal Text Processor", "Hardware Terminal Transfer Point"]'::jsonb, 0, 'HTTP stands for HyperText Transfer Protocol — the standard set of rules that web browsers and web servers use to request and deliver web pages and other resources over the internet.', 'Internet and web usage, email, instant messaging', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Which of the following best describes an email (electronic mail) system?', '["A service that lets users compose, send, and receive text messages (often with attachments) over a network, addressed to a specific electronic mailbox address", "A type of printer driver that translates documents into printable form", "A hardware component located physically inside the CPU chip itself", "A technique used to convert numbers between different number bases"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Email is a service for exchanging digital messages — including text and file attachments — between users over a network, with each message routed to a specific mailbox identified by an email address.', 'Internet and web usage, email, instant messaging', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Instant Messaging (IM) differs from email mainly in that IM is generally designed for:', '["sending large physical packages through a postal courier service", "near real-time, back-and-forth text conversation between users who are online at the same time", "permanently archiving files, with no communication feature involved at all", "printing documents remotely on a printer in another location"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Instant Messaging is designed for immediate, real-time text conversation between users who are simultaneously online, unlike email, which is typically used for less time-sensitive, asynchronous messages that may not be read immediately.', 'Internet and web usage, email, instant messaging', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In a typical domain name such as ''www.unn.edu.ng'', the part ''.edu'' generally indicates that the website belongs to:', '["a commercial business", "an educational institution", "a government military branch", "a personal blog with no organisational affiliation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ''.edu'' segment of a domain name is a top-level domain conventionally used to indicate that a website belongs to an educational institution, such as a university.', 'Internet and web usage, email, instant messaging', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A word processor application, such as one used to type, format, and print a letter or an assignment, is an example of which category of software?', '["an office application (application software)", "systems software that manages the computer''s own hardware", "firmware built permanently into a hardware chip", "a network protocol used to exchange data between computers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A word processor is application software — specifically an office application — designed to help users create, edit, format, and print text documents, distinct from systems software that manages the computer itself.', 'Office applications', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A spreadsheet application (such as one used to organise numbers into rows and columns and perform calculations) is primarily useful for:', '["browsing pages and resources on the World Wide Web", "organising data into a grid of cells and automatically performing calculations, such as sums, averages, and formulas, across that data", "playing video files stored on the computer''s hard disk", "compiling programming source code into machine language"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A spreadsheet application organises data into rows and columns (cells) and lets users apply formulas and functions — such as sums, averages, and more complex calculations — automatically across that data, making it especially useful for budgets, calculations, and data analysis.', 'Office applications', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A presentation application (such as one used to design and display slides during a talk) is primarily designed to help users:', '["manage a searchable database of a company''s customer records", "create a sequence of visual slides — combining text, images, and other media — to support an oral presentation to an audience", "convert numbers between binary and hexadecimal representations", "manage the settings and configuration of a computer''s operating system"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A presentation application allows the user to create a sequence of slides, typically combining text, images, charts, and other media, designed to be displayed to an audience to support a spoken talk or lecture.', 'Office applications', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A Database Management System (DBMS) is best described as software that allows users to:', '["define, create, maintain, and control access to a structured collection of related data (a database)", "only display static images stored on the computer''s hard disk", "physically manufacture the computer''s hardware components", "translate text from one human spoken language into another"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A Database Management System (DBMS) is software that lets users define the structure of a database, store and organise related data within it, retrieve and update that data, and control who is allowed to access or modify it.', 'Statistical package and database management system', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In a relational database, data is typically organised into structures made up of rows and columns, known as:', '["tables", "flowcharts", "menus", "registers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A relational database organises related data into tables, where each row represents a single record and each column represents a specific field/attribute of that record — related tables can then be linked together through common fields.', 'Statistical package and database management system', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'A statistical package (statistical software), as used in data analysis, is primarily designed to help users:', '["perform mathematical/statistical computations and analysis on data, such as computing averages, correlations, or running data models", "physically repair and replace faulty computer hardware components", "design flowchart shapes only, with no data-analysis features at all", "manage and regulate the electrical power supplied to the computer"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A statistical package is application software built specifically to help users analyse data using statistical and mathematical techniques — such as computing averages, standard deviations, correlations, or building data models — beyond what a basic spreadsheet easily supports.', 'Statistical package and database management system', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'One key advantage of using a proper Database Management System (DBMS) over storing related data in many separate, disconnected files is that a DBMS:', '["makes it completely impossible to ever update the stored data afterward", "helps reduce data duplication/inconsistency and provides controlled, structured access to related data through queries", "always requires far more storage space, with no other real benefit", "eliminates the need for any input devices connected to the computer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A DBMS helps reduce data duplication and inconsistency by centralising related data in a structured way, and it provides controlled, query-based access to that data — advantages that scattered, disconnected data files do not offer.', 'Statistical package and database management system', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The binary number system, used internally by digital computers, is a number system with base:', '["2", "8", "10", "16"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The binary number system has a base (radix) of 2, meaning it uses only two digits — 0 and 1 — to represent all values; this maps naturally onto a digital computer''s two physical states (on/off, high/low voltage).', 'Number systems', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Converting the decimal number 13 to binary gives:', '["1101", "1110", "1011", "1010"]'::jsonb, 0, '13 in decimal splits as 8+4+1 = 2^3+2^2+2^0, giving binary digits 1 (for 8), 1 (for 4), 0 (for 2), 1 (for 1) — that is, 1101 in binary. Checking: 1101(base 2) = 1×8 + 1×4 + 0×2 + 1×1 = 8+4+0+1 = 13.', 'Number systems', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The binary number 1010 is equivalent to which decimal value?', '["8", "9", "10", "12"]'::jsonb, 2, '1010 in binary is read right to left as: 0×1 + 1×2 + 0×4 + 1×8 = 0+2+0+8 = 10. So 1010(base 2) equals 10 in decimal.', 'Number systems', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The octal number system uses which base, and which set of digits?', '["base 8, using digits 0 through 7", "base 8, using digits 0 through 9", "base 10, using digits 0 through 8", "base 16, using digits 0 through 7"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The octal number system has base 8, and it uses exactly eight distinct digit symbols: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, and 7 — never 8 or 9, since those values would need to be represented using two digits (e.g. 8 in decimal is 10 in octal).', 'Number systems', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The hexadecimal number system, commonly used as a compact way to represent binary values, uses base:', '["8", "10", "16", "20"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The hexadecimal number system has base 16, using sixteen digit symbols: 0 through 9 for the first ten values, and the letters A, B, C, D, E, F to represent the values 10 through 15.', 'Number systems', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In hexadecimal notation, the letter ''C'' represents which decimal value?', '["10", "11", "12", "13"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In hexadecimal, the letters A–F extend the digit set beyond 9: A=10, B=11, C=12, D=13, E=14, F=15. So the hexadecimal digit ''C'' represents the decimal value 12.', 'Number systems', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Converting the decimal number 26 to octal (base 8) gives:', '["30", "32", "34", "22"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dividing 26 by 8 gives a quotient of 3 with a remainder of 2 (since 3×8=24, and 26−24=2). Reading the remainder then the final quotient gives 32 in octal. Checking: 32(base 8) = 3×8 + 2×1 = 24+2 = 26.', 'Number systems', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'In the 1''s complement method of representing signed binary numbers, the negative of a binary number is obtained by:', '["adding 1 to every bit of the original binary number in turn", "inverting (flipping) every bit of the original number — every 0 becomes 1 and every 1 becomes 0", "converting the number to decimal first and then multiplying it by −1", "removing the leftmost bit of the original number entirely"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The 1''s complement of a binary number is found by simply inverting every bit: each 0 is changed to a 1, and each 1 is changed to a 0. For example, the 1''s complement of 0101 is 1010.', 'Number systems', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'The 2''s complement of a binary number is obtained by:', '["taking the 1''s complement of the number and then adding 1 to the result", "leaving the original binary number completely unchanged throughout", "dividing the original binary number by 2 and discarding the remainder", "converting the binary number directly into its hexadecimal equivalent"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 2''s complement of a binary number is computed by first taking its 1''s complement (inverting every bit), and then adding 1 to that result. 2''s complement is the standard method computers use to represent negative numbers.', 'Number systems', false),
  ('cos-101-intro-computing', 'COS 101: Introduction to Computer Science', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Computer Science', '100 Level', 'Why do digital computers use the binary number system internally, rather than the decimal system humans normally use?', '["Because binary digits (0 and 1) map directly onto simple two-state electronic circuits, such as a transistor being on or off, which are far easier to build reliably than circuitry needing ten distinct states", "Because binary numbers always require noticeably fewer digits to write down than the equivalent decimal numbers do", "Because the earliest computer designers personally found decimal notation confusing and difficult to read on paper", "There is no real engineering reason behind the choice at all; it was simply an arbitrary early convention"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Binary''s two digits (0 and 1) correspond naturally to simple, reliable two-state electronic switching (such as a transistor being on/off, or a voltage being high/low), which is far more practical and less error-prone to build in hardware than circuitry that would need to distinguish ten different reliable states, as decimal would require.', 'Number systems', false);
